|term-group[binding][name][mint][maintainer][dangle][collide]
  :status proposed

  A **BINDING** is the first of the two mechanisms by which a @{reference} actually reaches its @{referents} (the other is @{match}): someone (the **MAINTAINER**) deliberately associates (**MINT**s) a **NAME** with a referent and keeps that association (binding) alive. Everything about a binding is a fact about that upkeep, not the name or the referent. The maintenance involved fails when the binding no longer reaches a referent, called a **DANGLE**, or when the maintainer has more than one mint of the same name (they **COLLIDE**).
  The name can be considered a new {1,1} reference to a referent that is more direct. The uniqueness of names is a *norm* a maintainer may police (cheapest at mint time) in order to avoid collision failures, instead of an invariant.

  |term[name      ] A repeatable token (e.g., a word, string, or number) that is recognizable wherever it is used.
  |term[mint      ] The creation of a binding, at a moment, by its first maintainer.
  |term[maintainer] Whoever minted a binding or inherits its upkeep (e.g., a system, person, family, filesystem, registry, community, ...).
  |term[dangle    ] The failure where a binding no longer reaches a referent — its upkeep has lapsed, or its referent has ceased (or moved) without the binding being updated.
  |term[collide   ] The failure where one maintainer holds more than one mint of the same name.
  |term[binding   ] A deliberate, maintained association from a name to a referent.
    :synonyms [declaration] ; the scope-graphs literature's word
    |rels :binds   @{name}       {1,1}
          :to      @{referent}   {1,1}
          :kept-by @{maintainer} {1,1}
    |invariants
      - The binding is the whole connection; no property of the referent participates, and none of the name's beyond recognizability
      - Its only failure modes in this definition are the maintenance failures: @{dangle} and @{collide}
  |discussion
    - Collision is never defined away and must be accounted for. Real systems have collisions and are judged by how they surface them.
    - "Joseph" in a large jurisdiction is a binding whose maintaining community is too large for the job; hence the rest of the address.
    - Where bindings live (the jurisdiction within which collisions are counted) stays informal until @{scope}.

  |examples
    |             Name            |          Maintainer            |
    |-----------------------------|--------------------------------|
    | Filename                    | The OS/filesystem              |
    | Phone-book entry            | The publisher                  |
    | Variable declaration        | The language scope it lives in |
    | "Joseph" within a household | The family                     |
    | A DNS record                | The zone's operator            |

  |working-notes
    - Hardest known stress case: Java packages "exist by virtue of use" — the namespace is constituted by the union of its users, which strains "kept by whoever minted it."
    - WRT collision: The scope-graphs calculus agrees — duplicate declarations stay representable precisely so that erroneous states (the normal case in tooling) remain describable. This is a deliberate divergence from a prior formulation that built "unique within parent" into the definitions.
